/**
 * Pose templates derived from real MediaPipe landmark data. 12 asanas.
 *
 * GENERATED FILE — do not hand-edit.
 *
 * Two sources, both MIT licensed:
 *  - hannalaguilar/yoga-classifier ships BlazePose keypoint CSVs: 1352
 *    captures of 6 asanas across 29 people. Used as published.
 *  - Manoj-2702/Yoga_Poses-Dataset ships images for 8 asanas. Its bundled
 *    CSVs are normalised 0-1 coordinates, which lose the frame's aspect
 *    ratio — combining them directly scored only 39% on a pose the model
 *    already knew. Landmarks were therefore re-extracted from the 484 source
 *    images at their true pixel dimensions, which is the geometry this
 *    embedding assumes.
 *
 * Six of Manoj's poses are new; its Vrikshasana and Trikonasana clusters are
 * appended to the existing tree/triangle entries instead, because stock
 * photography covers arm and leg variants the studio captures do not.
 *
 * Embedding: landmarks translated to the hip centre, scaled by
 * max(2.5 x torso, furthest landmark), reduced to 21 pairwise offset
 * vectors (x, y, z) = 63 dimensions. Classification is nearest-centroid.
 *
 * ACCURACY. 99.3% on the six poses that have person labels, measured
 * leave-persons-out over 5 folds while competing against all twelve classes.
 * That is up from 98.4% when the model held only six: the extra tree and
 * triangle variants took tree recall from 93% to 100%, which more than paid
 * for the six added classes.
 *
 * The six new poses measured 83-100% recall, but in-sample only — that
 * dataset has no person labels to group by, so those figures are optimistic
 * and are weaker evidence than the number above. Weakest is utkata_konasana
 * at 83%, confused with veerabhadrasana; goddess and warrior genuinely share
 * a wide-legged, bent-knee shape.
 */

/** Joint keys, in the order angles are stored in each cluster. */
